---
/**
 * Commercial license summary for the marketing landing.
 *
 * IMPORTANT — this is a *summary* of the license, not the legally
 * executed EULA. The full text of the LICENSE file in the repo is
 * what governs the actual sale; this page exists so prospective buyers
 * can read the gist without cloning the repo.
 *
 * Replace the values in the `terms` array with your own terms and
 * have the executed EULA reviewed by counsel before launch.
 */
import Legal from "@/layouts/legal.astro"
import { site } from "@/config/site"

const lastUpdated = "2026-05-01"
const contactEmail = site.social.email
---

<Legal
  title="License"
  description="A plain-English summary of the LaunchKit commercial license."
  eyebrow={`Effective · ${lastUpdated}`}
>
  <p>
    {site.brand} is sold under a <strong>commercial license</strong>:
    you buy once, you ship as many products as you want, you don't
    redistribute the boilerplate itself. The full executed EULA ships
    inside the repository as <code>LICENSE</code>; this page is the
    short version for prospective buyers.
  </p>

  <h2>You can</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Use the source code to build <strong>unlimited commercial
      applications</strong>, including paid SaaS, internal tools,
      one-off client work, and side projects.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Modify</strong> the code freely — rename files, swap
      stacks, delete features, brand it as your own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deploy</strong> compiled and source forms of your
      applications to any host, on any number of servers.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use it as the foundation for client deliverables. The license is
      tied to you; your client gets the application you build, not the
      boilerplate license itself.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>You cannot</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Resell or redistribute the boilerplate</strong> as a
      starter kit, template, code generator, or competing product.
    </li>
    <li>
      Publish, sublicense, or share the source code as-is to third
      parties — except as bundled inside an application you built.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use the license credentials of another buyer or transfer your
      license to a third party without our written consent.
    </li>
    <li>
      Remove the proprietary notices contained in the codebase
      (you can replace them with your own; you can't strip them
      without replacement in derivative re-distributions).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Updates and support</h2>
  <p>
    Each license tier sets the support window. The Standard tier
    includes one year of updates and access to the buyers'
    Discord; the Lifetime tier includes updates forever, priority
    support, a private channel, and early access to new features.
    Security fixes ship to <strong>all license holders</strong> for
    free, regardless of tier.
  </p>

  <h2>Refunds</h2>
  <p>
    Once you've been added to the private GitHub organization, the
    source code is in your hands — so we don't offer refunds on
    repositories that have been accessed. If you change your mind
    before we send the GitHub invite (typically the first few hours
    after checkout), email us within <strong>7 days</strong> of
    purchase and we'll refund in full.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fairest way to avoid buyer's regret is to read the full FAQ
    and email us with any questions <em>before</em> you check out.
    We're happy to walk you through specific parts of the codebase,
    share a Loom of the dashboard, or answer anything that's still
    unclear. We'd rather lose a sale than have you regret it.
  </p>

  <h2>Warranty and liability</h2>
  <p>
    The software is provided <em>as is</em>, without warranty of any
    kind, express or implied. To the maximum extent permitted by law,
    our total liability under this license is limited to the fees
    you paid for it. The full disclaimer is in the executed
    <code>LICENSE</code> file shipped with the codebase.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party dependencies</h2>
  <p>
    The codebase includes third-party open source software, each
    governed by its own license. You're responsible for complying
    with the terms of those components. The headline ones (Django,
    Next.js, React, Tailwind, shadcn/ui) are MIT or BSD-licensed and
    impose no additional restrictions on you.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    For licensing questions or enterprise terms, email{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>
</Legal>
